<!DOCTYPE html>
{#- data-presentation is the PRESENTATION MODE this edition asked for
    (lib/editions.js PRESENTATION_MODES, implemented in src/css/main.css).
    On <html> rather than <body> so the mode can move :root custom properties -
    including the root font-size, which a body-level attribute could not reach.
    Always emitted, since every edition record resolves a mode: "story" is a
    real value with no rules of its own rather than an absent one. -#}
<html lang="{{ site.language | default('en') }}" data-presentation="{{ edition.presentation }}">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  {#- READER'S PRESENTATION MODE, RESTORED BEFORE FIRST PAINT.

      This has to be inline and blocking. src/js/presentation.js is deferred,
      like every other script here, and a deferred script cannot do this job:
      the page would paint in the edition's own mode and then jump to the
      reader's when the type size changed under it. A visible reflow on every
      page load is worse than not offering the choice at all.

      Small enough to be cheap, and wrapped because localStorage ACCESS itself
      throws in some privacy modes rather than returning null. On any failure
      the attribute base.njk already wrote on <html> stands, which is the
      edition's mode - a correct page, just not this reader's preference.

      The key is duplicated in presentation.js and noted there. The valid list
      comes from lib/editions.js via .eleventy.js so the two cannot drift; an
      unrecognised stored value (a retired mode, a hand-edited key) is ignored
      rather than applied, since applying it would select no CSS at all. -#}
  <script>
    (function () {
      try {
        var stored = window.localStorage.getItem("fi:presentation-mode");
        if (stored && {{ presentationModeIds | dump | safe }}.indexOf(stored) !== -1) {
          document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-presentation", stored);
        }
      } catch (err) { /* storage unavailable - keep the edition's mode */ }
    })();
  </script>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
  <meta name="description" content="{{ description | default(site.description) }}" />
  {#- What this domain is serving; also at /version.txt. See src/_data/build.js. -#}
  <meta name="site-version" content="{{ build.version }}" />
  {%- if robots %}
  <meta name="robots" content="{{ robots }}" />
  {%- elif site.noindex %}
  {#- SITE_NOINDEX=true: a testing/staging domain noindexes every page,
      belt to robots.txt's Disallow braces. -#}
  <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow" />
  {%- endif %}
  <title>{% if title %}{{ title }} | {{ site.title }}{% else %}{{ site.title }}{% endif %}</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/star-rangers/css/main.css?v={{ build.assetVersion }}" />

  <link rel="icon" href="/star-rangers/images/icons/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
  <link rel="icon" href="/star-rangers/images/icons/favicon-32.png" sizes="32x32" type="image/png" />
  <link rel="icon" href="/star-rangers/images/icons/favicon-16.png" sizes="16x16" type="image/png" />
  <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/star-rangers/images/icons/favicon-180.png" />
  {#- SELF-canonical: every domain declares itself the canonical home of its own
      URLs, rather than pointing at sciencefiction.site.

      That is the right answer here specifically BECAUSE the domains are meant
      to diverge. A cross-domain canonical says "this page is a copy, rank the
      other one" - which consolidates ranking signals into one host and, in
      exchange, asks search engines not to rank the independently-branded
      domains at all. That trade is worth making for duplicates and is exactly
      wrong for editions that carry their own framing, cast and copy.

      Built from page.url + site.url, so it matches og:url above exactly and
      picks up SITE_DOMAIN per clone with no per-domain configuration.

      Deliberately NOT emitted on a page this build hides. `robots` is set to
      "noindex, follow" precisely then (see .eleventy.js for the layout-swapped
      pages and src/scene-pov.njk for the POV ones), and noindex plus canonical
      is contradictory signalling: one says don't index this, the other says
      index this URL instead. A placeholder has nothing worth canonicalising -
      the reader-facing pointer to the domain that does have the page is the
      link in its body. The two redirect stubs (glossary/dynarch.njk,
      lore/solar-system-concordant.njk) are unaffected: they hand-write their
      own head and keep canonical + noindex, which is the conventional pairing
      for a redirect rather than for a placeholder. -#}
  {#- `site.canonicalBase` is this build's own host EXCEPT on a domain that is
      a non-ranking member of a tier family, where it is the family's ranking
      host (lib/editions.js's `ranksAt`). So the self-canonical described above
      is still what the default, sciencefiction and pets editions emit - a
      family of one is its own canonical - and the fellowship, church-space and
      starquest siblings point at the one domain in their family that ranks.

      Note this DIVERGES from og:url below, which stays self-referential on
      purpose. The two answer different questions: rel=canonical tells a
      crawler which of several identical copies to index, while og:url is what
      a reader gets when they share the page they are actually looking at. A
      reader who shares a church-space page should hand their friend a
      church-space link, not a fellowship one - consolidating the search signal
      was the decision, rebranding the reader's own share was not. -#}
  {%- if (not robots or "noindex" not in robots) and not site.noindex %}
  <link rel="canonical" href="{{ page.url | absoluteUrl(site.canonicalBase) }}" />
  {%- endif %}
  <link rel="alternate" type="application/atom+xml" title="{{ site.name }} — New Episodes" href="{{ '/feed/feed.xml' | absoluteUrl(site.url) }}" />

  <meta property="og:site_name" content="{{ site.name }}" />
  <meta property="og:type" content="{{ ogType }}" />
  <meta property="og:title" content="{{ title | default(site.title) }}" />
  <meta property="og:description" content="{{ description | default(site.description) }}" />
  <meta property="og:url" content="{{ page.url | absoluteUrl(site.url) }}" />
  <meta property="og:image" content="{{ ogImage | absoluteUrl(site.url) }}" />
  {%- if ogImageAlt %}
  <meta property="og:image:alt" content="{{ ogImageAlt }}" />
  {%- endif %}
  {#- Omitted together when the file couldn't be measured: a guessed dimension
      makes a platform reserve a box the image doesn't fill. -#}
  {%- if ogImageSize %}
  <meta property="og:image:width" content="{{ ogImageSize.width }}" />
  <meta property="og:image:height" content="{{ ogImageSize.height }}" />
  {%- endif %}
  <meta property="og:locale" content="{{ site.ogLocale }}" />
  {#- Only for real article pages, and only where the front matter actually
      carries a `date`. The two halves of that test read from different places
      on purpose. `date` here is the raw front-matter value (a string like
      "2026-07-04"), so it answers "did the author set one?" - Eleventy always
      supplies page.date, falling back to file mtime, and testing that instead
      would stamp every lore entry with a publication time it never had. But
      the VALUE has to come from page.date, which is Eleventy's parsed Date;
      dateToRfc3339 uses DateTime.fromJSDate and renders the raw string as
      empty. Same reason feed.njk formats collection items' `.date`. -#}
  {%- if ogType == "article" and date %}
  <meta property="article:published_time" content="{{ page.date | dateToRfc3339 }}" />
  {%- endif %}
  <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
  <meta name="twitter:title" content="{{ title | default(site.title) }}" />
  <meta name="twitter:description" content="{{ description | default(site.description) }}" />
  <meta name="twitter:image" content="{{ ogImage | absoluteUrl(site.url) }}" />
  {%- if ogImageAlt %}
  <meta name="twitter:image:alt" content="{{ ogImageAlt }}" />
  {%- endif %}
</head>
<body>
  <a class="skip-link" href="#main-content">Skip to main content</a>

  <header class="site-header" role="banner">
    <div class="site-header__inner">
      <a class="site-logo" href="/star-rangers/">
        <span class="site-logo__icon" aria-hidden="true">✦</span>
        <span class="site-logo__text">{{ site.name }}</span>
      </a>
      <nav class="site-nav" aria-label="Main navigation">
        <ul class="site-nav__list" role="list">
          <li><a href="/star-rangers/" {% if page.url == "/star-rangers/" %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Home</a></li>
          {#- The Reading Plan is the front door for a first visit, and until
              2026-10-09 it was reachable only from the homepage's Begin
              Reading button; a site review that day found a new reader
              walking the catalogue sections instead. It sits second, before
              the shelves it introduces. -#}
          <li><a href="/star-rangers/start/" {% if "/start/" in page.url %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Start</a></li>
          <li><a href="/star-rangers/seasons/" {% if "/seasons/" in page.url %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Seasons</a></li>
          <li><a href="/star-rangers/threads/" {% if "/threads/" in page.url %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Threads</a></li>
          <li><a href="/star-rangers/characters/" {% if "/characters/" in page.url %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Characters</a></li>
          {#- A header item is a promise that the section behind it has
              something on it (the same rule the homepage cards follow). On a
              narrowed edition Lore, Atlas, Timeline, Glossary or Codex can be
              empty - undercover-pets.com had three items opening on "nothing
              published yet" - so each is shown only when its filtered
              collection is non-empty. Seasons, Threads, Characters, Story
              Engine and About always have a page behind them. 2026-09-13. -#}
          {%- set atlasHasPlaces = false -%}
          {%- for entry in collections.lore -%}
            {%- if entry.data.category == "Locations" and entry.data.galaxy %}{% set atlasHasPlaces = true %}{% endif -%}
          {%- endfor %}
          {% if collections.timelineEvents.length %}<li><a href="/star-rangers/timeline/" {% if "/timeline/" in page.url %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Timeline</a></li>{% endif %}
          {% if collections.lore.length %}<li><a href="/star-rangers/lore/" {% if "/lore/" in page.url %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Lore</a></li>{% endif %}
          {% if atlasHasPlaces %}<li><a href="/star-rangers/atlas/" {% if "/atlas/" in page.url %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Atlas</a></li>{% endif %}
          {% if collections.glossary.length %}<li><a href="/star-rangers/glossary/" {% if "/glossary/" in page.url %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Glossary</a></li>{% endif %}
          {% if collections.codex.length %}<li><a href="/star-rangers/codex/" {% if "/codex/" in page.url %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Codex</a></li>{% endif %}
          <li><a href="/star-rangers/story-engine/" {% if "/story-engine/" in page.url or "/journal/" in page.url %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Story Engine</a></li>
          <li><a href="/star-rangers/about/" {% if "/about/" in page.url %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}>About</a></li>
        </ul>
      </nav>
      <div class="site-search" data-pagefind-bundle="/star-rangers/pagefind/" data-pagefind-ignore>
        <form class="site-search__form" role="search" aria-label="Search the site">
          <input
            type="search"
            class="site-search__input"
            id="site-search-input"
            placeholder="Search…"
            autocomplete="off"
            aria-expanded="false"
            aria-controls="site-search-results"
          />
        </form>
        <div class="site-search__results" id="site-search-results" hidden></div>
      </div>
      {#- The reader-side presentation switcher. Rendered hidden and unhidden by
          src/js/presentation.js, so a reader with no JS sees no control at all
          rather than four buttons that do nothing. The page reads fine either
          way: what JS adds is the CHOICE, not the reading.

          In the header rather than the footer because it changes the whole
          site's posture and applies to every page, which is what site chrome
          is for - and because a reader who wants larger type wants it before
          reading, not after scrolling past everything they could not read.

          The options carry the modes' READER-FACING labels ("Large print",
          not "primer"): a reader on this domain is choosing a type size, not
          declaring an allegiance to another edition. See PRESENTATION_MODES. -#}
      <div class="reading-mode" data-presentation-ui hidden>
        <details class="reading-mode__details">
          <summary class="reading-mode__summary">Text size</summary>
          <div
            class="reading-mode__panel"
            data-presentation-switcher
            data-site-default="{{ edition.presentation }}"
            hidden
          >
            <p class="reading-mode__note" id="reading-mode-note">
              Type size and spacing. Kept in this browser only — nothing is sent anywhere,
              and it changes how the pages look, never what they say.
            </p>
            <div class="reading-mode__options" role="group" aria-labelledby="reading-mode-note">
              <button type="button" class="reading-mode__btn" data-presentation-mode="" aria-pressed="true">
                This site's setting<span class="reading-mode__btn-note"> ({{ presentationModes[edition.presentation].label }})</span>
              </button>
              {#- The edition's OWN mode is skipped here: the first button already is
                  it, and offering "Large print" beside "This site's setting (Large
                  print)" asks a reader to tell apart two buttons that do the same
                  thing to the page. The difference is real but it is a
                  maintainer's distinction (follow the edition vs pin a value),
                  and it survives where it matters - the first button clears the
                  stored preference, so a reader who never chose anything keeps
                  following this domain if its posture is ever changed. -#}
              {%- for id, mode in presentationModes %}
              {%- if id != edition.presentation %}
              <button type="button" class="reading-mode__btn" data-presentation-mode="{{ id }}" aria-pressed="false">
                {{ mode.label }}
              </button>
              {%- endif %}
              {%- endfor %}
            </div>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main id="main-content" class="main-content" data-pagefind-body>
    {{ content | safe }}

    {% if comments and commentsEnabled %}
    {#- The board is the PAGE's, not the build's: .eleventy.js's giscusBoard
        picks a thread's own board (church-space -> the Communion's) on any
        domain, and the build's board otherwise. Falls back to the global for
        a template that reaches here without the computed field. -#}
    {% set board = giscusBoard or giscus %}
    {% set commentsCategoryConfig = board.categories[commentsCategory] %}
    <section class="site-comments" aria-label="Comments" data-pagefind-ignore>
      <h2 class="site-comments__heading">Discussion</h2>
      {#- COLLAPSED BY DEFAULT, AND SAID TO BE MODERATED (Dermot's ruling,
          2026-09-04, story-bible/intake-2026-09-04.md, ninth message). giscus
          renders GitHub Discussions live and has no approval queue, so what
          this stack can honestly offer is (a) after-the-fact moderation from
          the comments repos, which the note below states and the repo watch
          delivers to his inbox, and (b) nothing on the page until a reader
          asks: the widget sits inside a closed <details>, so a reader who
          arrived by accident meets no comment they did not open. Hidden
          until OPENED, not until approved - the note does not claim more.
          data-loading="lazy" keeps the iframe from loading until the
          disclosure is near the viewport, which for a closed one is never
          until it is opened. The heading stays outside so the section is
          still announced and findable. -#}
      <details class="site-comments__toggle">
        <summary class="site-comments__summary">Show discussion</summary>
        <p class="site-comments__note">
          Comments are read after they are posted and anything unsuitable is removed.
          Posting needs a GitHub account; reading needs nothing.
        </p>
      <script src="https://giscus.app/client.js"
        data-repo="{{ board.repo }}"
        data-repo-id="{{ board.repoId }}"
        data-category="{{ commentsCategoryConfig.name }}"
        data-category-id="{{ commentsCategoryConfig.id }}"
        {% if comment_id %}
        data-mapping="specific"
        data-term="{{ comment_id }}"
        {% else %}
        data-mapping="pathname"
        {% endif %}
        {# Exact-title match, not a loose/substring search: without this, a
           chapter's own comment_id (e.g. "unauthorised-therefore") could
           loosely match a scene's, which is that same term plus a suffix
           (e.g. "unauthorised-therefore-scene-1-adaeze") - pulling in the
           wrong discussion instead of showing "no comments yet." Also what
           makes a stale comment_id (e.g. a deleted chapter's) fail
           gracefully: with no exact-title discussion to find, giscus just
           shows an empty thread rather than guessing at a near match. #}
        data-strict="1"
        data-reactions-enabled="1"
        data-emit-metadata="0"
        data-input-position="bottom"
        data-theme="dark"
        data-lang="en"
        data-loading="lazy"
        crossorigin="anonymous"
        async>
      </script>
      </details>
    </section>
    {% endif %}
  </main>

  <footer class="site-footer" role="contentinfo">
    <div class="site-footer__inner">
      <div class="site-theme-audio">
        <span class="site-theme-audio__label">Site theme — {{ edition.themeAudio.label }}</span>
        <audio class="site-theme-audio__player" src="/star-rangers/audio/{{ edition.themeAudio.file }}" controls loop preload="none">
          Your browser does not support the audio element.
        </audio>
      </div>
      <p>
        <span>{{ site.name }}</span> — A Multi-Viewpoint Novel.
        Copyright © {{ build.copyrightYears }} Dermot R. Cochran.
        Released under the <a href="/star-rangers/#license" rel="license">CC BY-NC-ND 4.0</a> licence.
        <a href="/star-rangers/official/">Official editions</a>.
        <a href="/star-rangers/tiers/">Reading tiers</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="site-lang-note">
        Written in English, its canonical language. Your browser can translate any page into
        another language — those translations are automatic and <strong>unofficial</strong>, and
        the English text is always the authority on canon.
        {#- The fan-works anchor exists only on the site-wide About; a tier
            with its own plain-register About (lib/editions.js `about`) has no
            such section, and the sentence is written for the adult reader
            in any case. -#}
        {%- if not edition.about %} A human translation offered as a
        <a href="/star-rangers/about/#fan-works">fan work</a> is welcome too.
        {%- endif %}
      </p>
    </div>
  </footer>

  <script src="/star-rangers/js/presentation.js?v={{ build.assetVersion }}" defer></script>
  <script src="/star-rangers/js/pov.js?v={{ build.assetVersion }}" defer></script>
  <script src="/star-rangers/js/search.js?v={{ build.assetVersion }}" defer></script>
</body>
</html>
